{"qid":"Q1529261","names":["Ottoman Tripolitania"],"title":"Ottoman Tripolitania","endonym":null,"capsule":"Ottoman Tripolitania, also known as the Regency of Tripoli (Trablusgarp Eyaleti, later Vilayet), constituted the Ottoman province covering roughly the territory of modern Libya from the reconquest of Tripoli from the Knights of St John in 1551. From 1711 to 1835 the province was effectively autonomous under the Karamanli dynasty, a local Arab-Ottoman family that pursued independent foreign policy including the First Barbary War with the United States (1801–1805); direct Ottoman rule was re-established by a naval intervention in 1835 that deposed the last Karamanli pasha. In the period covered by this dataset (from 1840), the province underwent Ottoman administrative modernization under the Tanzimat reforms, with Tripoli serving as the seat of an increasingly centralized vilayet. Italian forces invaded in September 1911, and by the Treaty of Ouchy (the First Treaty of Lausanne, October 1912) the Ottoman Empire formally ceded Tripolitania and Cyrenaica to Italy.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The provincial extent in the later Ottoman period is well-documented through administrative records and European diplomatic correspondence; the southern desert boundary is less precisely defined, as Ottoman administrative presence in the Fezzan was nominal.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Tripolitania","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Tripolitania"},{"label":"Britannica: Ottoman Empire","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Ottoman-Empire"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Ottoman Tripolitania","